  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains positioned over the region today, \nmaintaining dry and quiet conditions into early Monday. Patchy \nfog developed early this morning and is expected to redevelop \ntonight into Monday morning under light winds and limited \nmixing. While locally dense fog occurred earlier today, high-\nresolution ensemble guidance indicates low confidence in \nwidespread dense fog, with less than a 20% probability of \nvisibilities dropping below 3 miles.\n\nAttention quickly shifts to a fast-moving, relatively shallow \nPacific trough dropping into the Pacific Northwest on Monday. \nAhead of the boundary, breezy southwest-to-west winds will \ndevelop Monday afternoon before precipitation spreads into the \ncentral mountains late in the day. Showers will quickly expand \nacross eastern Idaho Monday night as the front pushes through, \naccompanied by gusty winds. Peak gusts between 30 and 40 mph are\nanticipated across the Snake River Plain overnight, though \nwinds are expected to remain below Wind Advisory thresholds. QPF\nguidance remains consistent with previous runs: lower \nelevations will generally see liquid amounts under 0.10\", while \nmountain zones range from 0.20\" to 0.30\". The Eastern Magic \nValley carries the highest likelihood (>85%) of staying dry, \nwhereas high terrain along the Continental Divide and between \nI-15 and the Wyoming border has the highest chance (>65%) of \nexceeding 0.25\" liquid. As colder air filters in behind the \nfront, snow levels will fall to around 6,500 feet overnight, \nyielding 1 to 3 inches of accumulation across mountain passes \nand peaks, encompassing the vast majority of ensemble spread. \nWhile a few stray flakes or a brief rain/snow mix may reach \nvalley floors after midnight, warm surface temperatures will \nprevent accumulation.\n\nAlthough the primary shortwave exits rapidly Tuesday afternoon \nwith most lower elevations drying out, eastern Idaho will remain\nunder the influence of a broader trough aloft through midweek. \nThis will support lingering showers across the higher terrain \nTuesday afternoon and Wednesday.\n\nEnsemble clusters remain in relatively good agreement regarding\nthe timing and depth of the next Pacific system, despite\ndifferences in the shape of the trough along the West Coast.\nEnsemble means indicate this trough will deepen along the\nPacific Northwest coast Thursday into Friday, spreading another\nround of widespread precipitation, falling temperatures, and\nhigh-elevation snow across the region to close the workweek.\nGiven the mean troughing remaining anchored over coastal states,\nunsettled conditions and scattered shower potential could linger\ninto the upcoming weekend. Precipitation amounts look light at\nthis time. The caveat will be how far off the coast the trough\ndigs, and how much dry southerly flow intrudes into the region\nbefore the trough can shift far enough inland.",
